  "walkthroughs": [
    {
      "tag": "Strong",
      "h2": "pH of strong acids",
      "body_html": "<p>Strong acids fully dissociate: HCl -> H+ + Cl-. So [H+] = [acid].</p><p>pH = -log10[H+]. Example: 0.01 mol/dm3 HCl -> pH = 2. Diprotic: 0.01 mol/dm3 H2SO4 -> [H+]=0.02 -> pH ≈ 1.7.</p>"
    },
    {
      "tag": "Strong base",
      "h2": "pH of strong bases",
      "body_html": "<p>NaOH -> Na+ + OH-. Use Kw: [H+] = Kw/[OH-].</p><p>Example: 0.1 mol/dm3 NaOH gives [H+]=1e-13, pH = 13.</p>"
    },
    {
      "tag": "Conjugate",
      "h2": "Spotting conjugate pairs",
      "body_html": "<p>NH3 + H2O <-> NH4+ + OH-. Conjugate pairs: NH3/NH4+ and H2O/OH-.</p><p>Strong acid has weak conjugate base; weak acid has stronger conjugate base. Water is amphiprotic.</p>"
    }
  ],
